  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let \\( d = \\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1) \\). By definition, \\( d \\) divides both \\( n + 7 \\) and \\( 2n + 1 \\).\n\nStep 2: Since \\( d \\) divides \\( n + 7 \\), we can express this as \\( n + 7 = kd \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nStep 3: Similarly, since \\( d \\) divides \\( 2n + 1 \\), we can express this as \\( 2n + 1 = ld \\) for some integer \\( l \\).\n\nStep 4: We can eliminate \\( n \\) by manipulating these equations. Multiply the first equation by 2 to get \\( 2n + 14 = 2kd \\).\n\nStep 5: Subtract the second equation from this new equation to eliminate \\( 2n \\): \\( (2n + 14) - (2n + 1) = 2kd - ld \\).\n\nStep 6: Simplify the left side: \\( 13 = (2k - l)d \\).\n\nStep 7: This shows that \\( d \\) must divide 13. The divisors of 13 are 1 and 13.\n\nStep 8: To determine if \\( d = 13 \\) is possible, check if there exists an \\( n \\) such that both \\( n + 7 \\) and \\( 2n + 1 \\) are multiples of 13.\n\nStep 9: Set \\( n + 7 = 13m \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Then \\( n = 13m - 7 \\).\n\nStep 10: Substitute \\( n = 13m - 7 \\) into \\( 2n + 1 \\): \\( 2(13m - 7) + 1 = 26m - 14 + 1 = 26m - 13 \\).\n\nStep 11: Notice that \\( 26m - 13 = 13(2m - 1) \\), which is clearly a multiple of 13.\n\nStep 12: Therefore, \\( d = 13 \\) is indeed possible.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{13}<|im_end|>",
