  "source": {
    "answer": "13",
    "level": 4,
    "problem": "Let $n$ be a positive integer.  What is the greatest possible value of $\\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$?",
    "solution": "Let $d = \\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$, so $d$ divides both $n + 7$ and $2n + 1$.  Then $d$ divides $2(n + 7) - (2n + 1) = 13$, so $d$ is at most 13.\n\nIf $n = 6$, then $\\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1) = \\gcd(13,13) = 13$, which shows that the value of 13 is attainable.  Therefore, the greatest possible value of $\\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$ is $\\boxed{13}$.",
